# PulsePlate Project Rules for Cursor AI

## Project Overview

PulsePlate is a FastAPI-based nutrition and meal planning application with VIP features for micronutrient optimization, auto-repair of meal plans, and regional shopping lists.

## Key Technologies

- FastAPI 0.116.1
- Pydantic 2.11.7
- Python 3.13.5
- pytest with coverage (target: 97%)
- Black formatter (line-length: 100)
- Ruff linter
- Hypothesis for property-based testing

## Project Structure

```
PulsePlate/
├── app/                    # FastAPI application
│   ├── routers/           # API endpoints
│   ├── schemas/           # Pydantic models
│   └── services/          # Business logic
├── core/                  # Core business logic
│   ├── targets.py         # Nutrition targets and profiles
│   ├── menu_engine.py     # Meal planning engine
│   └── recommendations.py # Nutrition recommendations
├── tests/                 # Test suite
└── data/                  # Data files (CSV, JSON)
```

## Coding Standards

### Python Style

- Use Black formatter with line-length=100
- Use Ruff for linting
- Follow PEP 8 with exceptions for line length
- Use type hints for all function parameters and return values
- Use Pydantic v2 syntax (model_dump(), model_validate())

### Testing

- Maintain 97% test coverage
- Use pytest for unit tests
- Use Hypothesis for property-based testing
- Mock external dependencies
- Test both success and error paths
- Use descriptive test names

### API Design

- Use FastAPI with Pydantic models
- Include proper HTTP status codes
- Use API key authentication for VIP endpoints
- Include comprehensive error handling
- Document endpoints with OpenAPI

### VIP Features

- VIP_MODULE_ENABLED feature flag controls VIP functionality
- MicronutrientTargets for enhanced nutrition goals
- Auto-repair functionality for meal plans
- Regional shopping lists with package rounding
- Recipe generation based on meal plans

## Development Workflow

### Before Committing

1. Run all tests: `python -m pytest tests -q`
2. Check coverage: `python -m pytest --cov=. --cov-fail-under=97 -q`
3. Run linting: `python -m ruff check . --fix`
4. Format code: `python -m black . --line-length=100`

### Test Commands

- `pptest` - Run all tests
- `ppcov` - Run tests with coverage
- `pplint` - Run linting
- `ppformat` - Format code
- `ppcheck` - Full quality check

### Environment Variables

- `VIP_MODULE_ENABLED=true` - Enable VIP features
- `PYTHONPATH` - Includes project root, core, app, tests

## Sprint Progress

- ✅ Sprint 0: VIP Module Foundation
- ✅ Sprint 1: Micronutrient Targets and Auto-repair
- 🔄 Sprint 2: Shoplist with package rounding
- ⏳ Sprint 3: Region Catalog (ES/US)
- ⏳ Sprint 4: Recipe Synthesis
- ⏳ Sprint 5: Auto-repair UX loop
- ⏳ Sprint 6: i18n, currency, PDF export

## Current Priorities

1. Fix test_app_coverage_final.py (red files issue)
2. Reach 97% test coverage
3. Complete Sprint 2: Shoplist functionality
4. Ensure all tests pass before any git push

## Important Notes

- Never push code without running full test suite
- Always check coverage before committing
- Use feature flags for new functionality
- Maintain backward compatibility
- Document all API changes
- Use proper error handling and logging

## File Naming Conventions

- Test files: test_*.py
- Core modules: snake_case
- API endpoints: kebab-case in URLs
- Configuration files: .env, pyproject.toml, pytest.ini

## Error Handling

- Use HTTPException for API errors
- Log errors with appropriate levels
- Provide meaningful error messages
- Handle edge cases gracefully
- Use try-catch blocks for external API calls
